<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Belisarius</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 500–<a href="/years/565">565</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Belisarius was the leading general of the Byzantine emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justinian-i">Justinian I</a>. He defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> at Dara in 530, helped crush the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nika-riots">Nika riots</a> in 532, destroyed the Vandal Kingdom in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> in 533–534 and led the long reconquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostrogoths">Ostrogoths</a>, taking Ravenna in 540. In old age he repelled the Kutrigur raid on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in 559.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/belisarius.jpg" alt="Belisarius" width="320" height="396" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Petar Milošević · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Belisarius_mosaic.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 533–534 the Byzantine Empire under Justinian sent <b>Belisarius</b>, who defeated the Vandals and restored imperial rule; a province called Africa Proconsularis existed again within the Byzantine Praetorian Prefecture of Africa until the Arab conquest of Carthage in 698.</blockquote><a href="/empire/africa-proconsularis">Africa Proconsularis · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In 533 <b>Belisarius</b> beat the Vandals at the Battle of Ad Decimum and the Battle of Tricamarum.</blockquote><a href="/empire/vandal-kingdom">Vandal Kingdom · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>During the Iberian War, a Sasanian Persian army defeated the Byzantine army of <b>Belisarius</b> at Callinicum (Raqqa), in northern Syria, on Easter Saturday.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-19">19 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>West of Carthage, the Byzantine army of <b>Belisarius</b> defeated the Vandal king Gelimer, whose brother Tzazon was killed.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-15">15 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Having met Ostrogothic resistance only at Panormus, which fell after a quick siege, the Byzantine general <b>Belisarius</b> completed the conquest of Sicily and made a triumphal entry into Syracuse at the end of his consular year.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-31">31 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The citizens of Rome, mindful of the fate of Naples, put up no resistance to Justinian's general <b>Belisarius</b>, and the 4,000-strong Gothic garrison withdrew.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-9">9 December in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Sapareva Banya, in what is now <a href="/countries/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=42.29,23.26&amp;year=542" aria-label="Sapareva Banya on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5114 659 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Belisarius"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5564 929h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5564 929h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=42.29,23.26&amp;year=542">What was here in 542 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…while human affairs follow whatever course may be in accordance with God's will, yet those who are in charge of any enterprise always win praise or blame according to their own deeds.&#8221;</p><footer>Letter to Justinian from besieged Rome, 537 (Procopius, History of the Wars, book 5, chapter 24) &#183; tr. H. B. Dewing, 1919 &#183; the letter as Procopius, his secretary, gives it &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/20298/pg20298-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;For achievements which transcend the nature of things may not properly and fittingly be ascribed to man's valour, but to a stronger power.&#8221;</p><footer>Letter to Justinian from besieged Rome, 537 (Procopius, History of the Wars, book 5, chapter 24) &#183; tr. H. B. Dewing, 1919 &#183; the letter as Procopius, his secretary, gives it &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/20298/pg20298-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;But I say to you that there will come a time when you will want to hide your heads under the thistles but will find no shelter anywhere.&#8221;</p><footer>Reply to the Gothic envoys at Rome, 537 (Procopius, History of the Wars, book 5, chapter 20) &#183; tr. H. B. Dewing, 1919 &#183; Procopius' account; he was Belisarius' secretary &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/20298/pg20298-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>3 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;You, however, act as though you were playing at dice, and want to risk all on a single cast; but it is not my custom to choose the short course in preference to the advantageous one.&#8221;</p><footer>Words to the people of Rome, impatient for battle, 537 (Procopius, History of the Wars, book 5, chapter 28) &#183; tr. H. B. Dewing, 1919 &#183; Procopius' account; he was Belisarius' secretary &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/20298/pg20298-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…we on our side permit the Goths to have the whole of Britain, which is much larger than Sicily and was subject to the Romans in early times.&#8221;</p><footer>Reply to the Gothic envoys during the siege of Rome, 537 (Procopius, History of the Wars, book 6, chapter 6) &#183; tr. H. B. Dewing, 1919 &#183; Procopius' account; the Goths had offered to give up Sicily &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/20298/pg20298-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Although your promise gave us to understand that your words would be brief and temperate, yet your discourse has been both long and not far from fraudulent in its pretensions.&#8221;</p><footer>Reply to the Gothic envoys during the siege of Rome, 537 (Procopius, History of the Wars, book 6, chapter 6) &#183; tr. H. B. Dewing, 1919 &#183; Procopius' account; he was Belisarius' secretary &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/20298/pg20298-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
